1
00:00:03,287 --> 00:00:08,417
In 1994, Peter Shor showed that a quantum computer could factor huge numbers,

2
00:00:08,417 --> 00:00:10,837
and break the encryption that protects the internet.

3
00:00:10,837 --> 00:00:15,267
Thirty years later, no quantum computer has done it at a useful scale.

4
00:00:15,267 --> 00:00:16,867
The reason is noise.

5
00:00:17,653 --> 00:00:18,499
We are Team PhASE,

6
00:00:18,499 --> 00:00:22,379
and Below Threshold is an interactive playground that takes you from a single qubit,

7
00:00:22,379 --> 00:00:26,813
through Shor's algorithm, to the error correction that keeps it alive.

8
00:00:27,553 --> 00:00:32,523
Every chapter runs on an exact quantum simulator, right in your browser.

9
00:00:32,523 --> 00:00:34,613
You do not read about superposition:

10
00:00:34,613 --> 00:00:38,161
you build it, measure it, and watch interference cancel.

11
00:00:38,920 --> 00:00:43,490
At the heart of Shor's algorithm is the quantum Fourier transform.

12
00:00:43,490 --> 00:00:47,100
Step through the real gate circuit, one gate at a time,

13
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:51,250
and watch a repeating pattern turn into sharp peaks.

14
00:00:52,020 --> 00:00:54,206
Then factor fifteen end to end:

15
00:00:54,206 --> 00:01:00,225
superposition, modular exponentiation, the Fourier transform, continued fractions,

16
00:01:00,225 --> 00:01:03,672
and out come the factors, three times five.

17
00:01:04,420 --> 00:01:05,694
Now add noise.

18
00:01:05,694 --> 00:01:10,947
On ten simulated qubits, the peaks blur, and the answer is lost.

19
00:01:10,947 --> 00:01:15,374
Real qubits fail roughly once in every thousand operations.

20
00:01:16,120 --> 00:01:22,120
Quantum error correction spreads one qubit across many, and asks them whether they agree,

21
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:23,902
without ever looking at the answer.

22
00:01:23,902 --> 00:01:29,070
The lit checks are clues, and a decoder has to work out what went wrong.

23
00:01:29,820 --> 00:01:31,280
So you play the decoder.

24
00:01:31,280 --> 00:01:36,646
Errors are hidden; only the alarms light up, like real hardware.

25
00:01:36,646 --> 00:01:41,058
A guided first level makes it playable with no background,

26
00:01:41,058 --> 00:01:45,220
and then a real matching decoder solves the same puzzle.

27
00:01:45,987 --> 00:01:47,370
Here is the twist.

28
00:01:47,370 --> 00:01:51,655
A real chip produces these puzzles a million times a second.

29
00:01:51,655 --> 00:01:57,057
Our real-time mode measures each decoder live in your browser:

30
00:01:57,057 --> 00:02:03,126
exact matching falls behind, the backlog grows, and the computation stalls.

31
00:02:03,887 --> 00:02:09,295
In the lab, real Monte Carlo runs let you watch the threshold appear:

32
00:02:09,295 --> 00:02:11,184
below it, bigger codes win.

33
00:02:11,184 --> 00:02:14,807
And a narrated film walks through every idea.

34
00:02:15,553 --> 00:02:17,336
None of it is faked.

35
00:02:17,336 --> 00:02:24,675
Our matching decoder finds the same minimum as PyMatching on all ninety thousand test problems,

36
00:02:24,675 --> 00:02:30,363
our code layout matches Stim, our thresholds land on the published values,

37
00:02:30,363 --> 00:02:35,151
and close to two hundred automated tests check the physics and the app.

38
00:02:35,151 --> 00:02:38,343
We even list exactly what we simplify.

39
00:02:39,087 --> 00:02:42,294
Shor's algorithm needs qubits that survive.

40
00:02:42,294 --> 00:02:45,807
That takes error correction, and decoders that keep up.

41
00:02:45,807 --> 00:02:48,627
Come and play, and keep it below threshold.
